# Line endings are LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Why this file exists, concretely: git's `core.autocrlf` defaults to `true` on Windows, and GitHub's
# windows-latest runner inherits that. Without this file a checkout there rewrites every text file to
# CRLF, while the layout generator writes LF — so `check:layouts` reported all five generated files
# as stale on Windows and only on Windows. The generated layouts were fine; the checkout had edited
# them.
#
# `eol=lf` (rather than plain `text=auto`) is the part that matters: it normalises the WORKING TREE,
# not just what is stored. Any tool that compares generated output against a committed file needs the
# two to be produced the same way, and that comparison is the whole point of a staleness check.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched. `vendor/` is ignored today, but an ignore rule is a decision that
# can be revisited, whereas corrupting a shared library by newline conversion is not recoverable by
# inspection — it just stops loading.
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.a    binary
*.lib  binary
*.zip  binary
*.wasm binary
*.png  binary
